<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Poonch State</h1><p class="st-meta">1850 CE – 1947 CE (approx.) · capital Poonch</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Dogra principality subordinate to Jammu and Kashmir, whose 1947 rebellion led to the creation of Azad Kashmir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1850 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> granted Poonch to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moti-singh-of-poonch">Moti Singh</a>, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh">Dhian Singh</a>, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir</a>. Its rulers held the title of Raja.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rajas governed a hilly region with a largely Muslim population, many of whom served in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir</a> repeatedly sought to curtail Poonch's autonomy, and in 1936 its subordinate status was confirmed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1947 Muslim ex-servicemen in Poonch rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Maharaja Hari Singh's</a> government, a rebellion that merged into the war over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. The district was divided by the 1949 ceasefire line between Pakistani-administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azad-kashmir">Azad Kashmir</a> and Indian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1850 CE – 1947 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.52%;width:1.93%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1850 CE – 1947 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Poonch</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moti Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam; Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pahari, Urdu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Poonch, Punch</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=poonch-state" aria-label="Poonch State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6570 953 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Poonch State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6890 1209L6905 1198"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6905 1198h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6890 1209h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6890 1209h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Poonch. <a href="/atlas?year=1898&amp;focus=poonch-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

